00:05:29.726 --> 00:05:31.730
So like I said,
our primary focus

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:34.380
is around veterinary
diagnostic equipment.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:37.530
And a few years ago, we
started to invest heavily

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:40.003
in machine learning technology
specifically around imaging.

00:05:40.003 --> 00:05:42.420
And I want to talk about a few
of these different products

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:43.590
that we have.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:46.890
The top left is
a SediVue device.

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:50.820
This analyzes raw urine and
takes microscopic images

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:51.780
of the urine.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:54.450
And then we use machine learning
to detect elements within that.

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:57.657
So that's things like red
blood cells, white blood cells,

00:05:57.657 --> 00:05:59.490
and a variety of different
types of crystals

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:01.740
that you can see in urine.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:06.750
We have 250 million images
from veterinary practices

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:08.060
in our cloud right now.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:11.160
We're on our fourth major
iteration of our machine

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.030
learning model.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:14.090
The last model we
added the ability

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:16.320
to detect a very
rare crystal that we

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.960
had to use machine learning
to analyze our data set

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:22.890
to find data to build up our
training sets to release that.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:26.620
We have hematology devices.

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:29.790
We also have a
pathology scanner.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.010
This generates about
two petabytes of imagery

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:35.070
every single year
that we can analyze.

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:35.820
We have radiology.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:37.890
I'll talk a little
about that more later.

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:40.220
We also sell an ECG device.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:41.970
We bundle that with a
subscription service

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.510
for veterinary practices.

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:45.960
This is frequently
used for screenings

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:50.062
before surgeries where a lot
of cases are normal hearts.

00:06:50.062 --> 00:06:52.020
They want to know that
the pet's healthy enough

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:54.150
to go under surgery.

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.460
So we use technology to
automatically read those.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:58.980
If it's normal, we'll send
that result automatically back

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:00.000
to the practice.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.930
If it's abnormal, we'll
have a cardiologist read it.

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:07.100
Cardiologists love this feature,
because reading normal ECGs

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:09.380
all day long is very boring.

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:11.510
They want to be able
to focus their efforts

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:16.180
on more interesting cases.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:19.630
The other device we
sell is a snap kit.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:21.850
These are kits we
sell for a long time.

00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:24.227
And essentially, what
they do is detect diseases

00:07:24.227 --> 00:07:26.560
in cats and dogs, things like
Lyme disease or heartworm.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:31.180
And it's a snap kit because you
put some fluid in there, blood

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:33.890
serum, and then you snap it.

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:37.070
And that result takes a
few minutes to show up.

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:39.290
And it's only valid for so long.

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:41.105
So what we did is we
developed this device,

00:07:41.105 --> 00:07:44.270
the snap pro reader, where
the veterinary technician can

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:45.710
put the snap kit in.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:48.140
It will actually automatically
snap it for them,

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:52.760
set off a timer, and then
take a picture of the window

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:55.005
at the right time.

00:07:55.005 --> 00:07:56.630
It reads the dot on
that to see if it's

00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:58.520
positive for any of the diseases
and then it automatically

00:07:58.520 --> 00:07:59.770
imports that into our systems.

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:10.420
We have a full suite
of radiology equipment.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:14.115
But we sell both the
capture equipment.

00:08:14.115 --> 00:08:15.990
Once you capture an
image, that automatically

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:18.675
goes up to our Web
PACS cloud system.

00:08:18.675 --> 00:08:21.750
We have 100 million
radiographs there.

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:25.350
Most veterinary practices
have an X-ray capture machine,

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:28.523
but they don't have a
radiologist on site.

00:08:28.523 --> 00:08:29.940
So we also provide
a service where

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:31.860
practices can send
us a radiograph

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:35.190
and we'll have radiologists
read them for them.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:38.017
And then finally, that
reports and the image

00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:40.100
will go back to their
practice management software

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.171
automatically.

00:08:43.445 --> 00:08:45.070
So I want to drill
in a little bit more

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:46.900
into the telemedicine
workflow, which

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.370
is where I see a
lot of opportunity

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:51.340
in machine learning technology.

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:54.398
A typical flow or
typical radiologist

00:08:54.398 --> 00:08:55.690
will have a couple of monitors.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:58.447
On the first monitor, they'll
have their case work list

00:08:58.447 --> 00:09:00.280
where they'll have a
list of all their cases

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:02.800
they need to work on that
day, some of the case details,

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.320
and then an image view
on monitors two and three

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:07.850
for example.

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:09.600
But one of the things
that they like to do

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:13.270
is they were taught to read
x-rays in a certain order.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:15.960
So for example, they like to
see their thorax images first,

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:19.710
and their abdomen images next.

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:21.420
So they spend time
the very beginning

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:23.970
organizing all their
data, generally

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:27.860
about 30 seconds for each case.

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:30.350
And right now we're at capacity.

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:32.480
We have several
hundred radiologists.

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:34.730
And the only way we can
really expand our business

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:37.940
is by adding more
radiologists or making

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:39.575
their workflow more efficient.

00:09:39.575 --> 00:09:40.950
And we've really
hired almost all

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:44.455
of the veterinary radiologists
there are already.

00:09:44.455 --> 00:09:47.000
So one of the challenges
is all of our images

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:50.480
come in somewhat unorganized.

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:52.220
They do have DICOM
tags generally.

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:53.460
But not always.

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:54.940
Sometimes they're also wrong.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:58.400
In the veterinary practice, the
technicians that capture x-rays

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:00.020
are not always highly trained.

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:01.980
And it can be a real
challenge sometimes

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:05.370
to get a proper x-ray
of a pet on the table.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:08.240
So they'll come in upside
down, flipped, sometimes

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.520
with a title,
sometimes it's blank.

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:13.610
So again, radiology will
spend that 30 seconds or so

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:17.490
on every single case just
organizing their images.

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:20.420
So we spend over half a
million dollars a year

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:23.345
paying radiologists just
to organize their images.

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:30.750
So the obvious solution is to
use machine learning technology

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:33.750
to help them and organize their
data so that when they open up

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:36.420
their case, their data is
all organized automatically

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:38.780
for them.

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:41.310
We started looking at this
maybe last year or so.

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:44.880
And we started looking at whole
image classification first.

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:46.880
So can we take these
images and just bucket them

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:48.830
into high level
pieces of anatomy--

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:53.295
for example skull, thorax,
abdomen, or extremity?

00:10:53.295 --> 00:10:54.420
And that works really well.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.960
It was very fast and easy
to sort of train whole image

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:59.720
classification.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:01.607
We did have a lot of
data that was labeled.

00:11:01.607 --> 00:11:03.440
So we kind of just put
those all in folders.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:05.773
It was very easy to set up
the training and the labeling

00:11:05.773 --> 00:11:08.750
of this and build a model.

00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:11.060
But one of the
challenges we saw was we

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:13.550
have a variety of images like
this, where it doesn't neatly

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.500
fit into any single bucket.

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:17.870
It's not just a skull, or
a thorax, or an abdomen.

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:20.180
It really has
components of all those.

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:23.840
And because again, a lot
of veterinary technicians

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:25.970
aren't highly trained,
they might take images

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:29.530
like this versus sort of
just the focused anatomy.

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:35.010
So this is where the object
detection capability of AutoML

00:11:35.010 --> 00:11:37.470
comes in, where
we can now detect

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:39.330
individual pieces
of the anatomy,

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:41.130
and label, and know
where they are.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:43.860
So with this, I can now take
this image and say well,

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:45.990
the intention of
this was a thorax.

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:47.730
It's what's in the
center of the image.

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:49.680
They weren't intending
to take a skull shot.

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:54.417
It's on the edge of the image.

00:11:54.417 --> 00:11:56.000
So our first pass
of this was actually

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:59.370
before object detection
in AutoML was available.

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:04.010
So we hired some temps,
and labeled all this data,

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:07.310
and built tools to do all this.

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:10.610
And in reality, that
cycle is very long.

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:13.640
We built our own labeling tool.

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:16.160
We bundled that together
with some TensorFlow models.

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:17.000
We evaluated that.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.790
We gave that to some
interns to label the data.

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:23.010
And this whole process was
many weeks to months long,

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:24.230
to be honest.

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:27.000
Because by the time you get
that label data, look at it,

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:31.040
analyze it, go look at
which ones were incorrect,

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:33.050
getting good visualization
of what's wrong

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:35.330
and where it's going wrong--

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:37.820
without sort of an
integrated tool like object

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:42.600
detection is a real challenge.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:45.460
So I want to show
this to you live--

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:47.920
a demo of what we did with
Auto Detection in AutoML.

00:12:53.750 --> 00:12:56.480
So the first part that you
do with Auto Detection AutoML

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:57.830
is upload your data set.

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:00.445
I'm going to skip this
part today just for time,

00:13:00.445 --> 00:13:02.570
but generally what you do
is just load up your data

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:04.760
into Google Cloud Storage.

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:07.970
You have the ability
to either upload labels

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:11.410
yourself or you can use the
labeling tool within Google

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:14.667
Auto Detection, depending on
if you already have labels

00:13:14.667 --> 00:13:15.500
from another system.

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:25.860
So for this example, we
uploaded 15,000 images

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:27.880
and did about nine
different classes.

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:30.990
So the classes are over
here-- abdomen, lateral

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:33.750
or [INAUDIBLE] were at two
different angles, extremity,

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:36.080
pelvis, skull, and whatnot.

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:42.990
This is what the
labeling looks like.

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:48.420
So over here is the skull, the
thorax, cavity, the abdomen,

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:49.520
the pelvis.

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:51.620
And if I want to
add another label,

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:53.540
you just simply draw a box.

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:55.730
And it adds as a
new entry over here.

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:57.500
And you pick what the label is.

00:14:00.332 --> 00:14:04.480
So it's really fast and easy
to add labels to your system.

00:14:13.382 --> 00:14:15.090
Once you've uploaded
your data and you've

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:18.745
labeled all your images,
the next step is to train.

00:14:18.745 --> 00:14:19.870
This does take a few hours.

00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:22.352
So I'm not going
to show this live.

00:14:22.352 --> 00:14:23.310
But it's pretty simple.

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:26.690
You just hit Train New Model,
and then hit Start Training.

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:35.452
Once your model
has been trained,

00:14:35.452 --> 00:14:37.660
then you still need to go
through an evaluation phase

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:38.780
to evaluate it.

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:39.280
What worked?

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:41.380
What didn't work?

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:43.990
Usually I'll do that by
looking at individual classes.

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:46.490
You can look at all
of them together.

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:50.560
But for example, if we look
at skull VDs, what it shows

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:53.055
you first is your
true positives.

00:14:53.055 --> 00:14:54.430
And what's really
nice about this

00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:57.550
is just the visualization
of this piece integrated

00:14:57.550 --> 00:14:59.360
into the tool.

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:03.850
So the green box is what
the model predicted,

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:05.740
and the gray box is what
our ground truth is,

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:07.700
or what our human
labelers labeled that.

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:14.020
It also shows false negatives.

00:15:14.020 --> 00:15:17.150
So this is areas where the
model failed to predict

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:18.747
the correct bounding box.

00:15:18.747 --> 00:15:20.330
Bounding box detection
is a little bit

00:15:20.330 --> 00:15:22.038
different than whole
image classification

00:15:22.038 --> 00:15:25.970
because the bounding
box that you labeled

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:28.370
is not going to be exactly
the same as the one the model

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:31.230
predicted pixel for pixel.

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:34.280
So Google and many
other libraries

00:15:34.280 --> 00:15:36.320
use what's called
intersection over union,

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:40.430
which is essentially how much
the two boxes overlap for that.

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:42.560
So in this example,
these are ones that it's

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:44.000
saying wasn't really right.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:47.055
But in reality, it just
didn't overlap enough.

00:15:47.055 --> 00:15:48.680
You do have the
ability to come in here

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:49.763
and change that threshold.

00:15:49.763 --> 00:15:51.650
So I can lower
that a little bit.

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:55.710
That will raise our accuracy
and sort of change results

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:56.210
down here.

00:16:01.342 --> 00:16:03.550
The other thing I'll show
you is the false positives,

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:07.220
so where your human
labeler didn't say there

00:16:07.220 --> 00:16:11.040
was something in there, but
the model found something.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:13.140
And in reality, in
our example, this

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:16.230
is actually cases where the
human labeler missed a label.

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:18.300
And we need to go
fix these labels.

00:16:21.928 --> 00:16:23.720
Once you've gone through
a couple of cycles

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:27.230
of labeling and training,
you automatically

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:29.240
get a model deployed.

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:32.503
It comes with some sample
code, which is simply REST API,

00:16:32.503 --> 00:16:34.670
or you can use cURL or
Python, whatever language you

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:36.380
want to use.

00:16:36.380 --> 00:16:39.200
It also has a really simple
way to test your model

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:40.770
on other data.

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:44.450
So for example, here's uploading
an image and predicting this.

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:45.950
So that was done
sort of on the fly.

00:16:51.533 --> 00:16:52.950
So the really nice
part about this

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:56.760
is none of these steps
required any code.

00:16:56.760 --> 00:16:59.950
So you don't really need to be
a data scientist or developer

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:04.260
to get a custom trained
model on your own problem

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:07.950
and a model deployed in the
cloud, which is really awesome.

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:10.140
You've taken what
we had before, which

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:13.290
is weeks and months of effort,
lots of development effort,

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:18.934
to something that now maybe a
business analyst could even do.

00:17:18.934 --> 00:17:22.339
Can we switch back to slides?

00:17:22.339 --> 00:17:25.204
So just to summarize,
AutoML significantly

00:17:25.204 --> 00:17:26.579
streamlined our
process for this.

00:17:26.579 --> 00:17:29.850
Again, we went from weeks down
to a couple of days to do this.

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:34.500
Our focus is really not
replacing radiologists.

00:17:34.500 --> 00:17:36.560
But how can we make
them more efficient?

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:38.930
How can we streamline
their process

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:41.010
and make their work
a little bit easier?

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:46.230
One of the key pieces
I do want to emphasize

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:49.140
is trying to integrate
machine learning models

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:52.500
into their existing workflow.

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:55.300
So as you think about problems
that you want to solve,

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:57.930
what are ones that can be
integrated into your workflow

00:17:57.930 --> 00:17:59.207
and not be separate?

00:17:59.207 --> 00:18:01.290
If I have to leave the
application that I normally

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:03.780
use and go to someone
else or there's more steps

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:06.540
to be able to
utilize that model,

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:09.300
that might not be a great use
case for machine learning.

00:18:12.010 --> 00:18:15.760
And then the other part to this
is this is a simple example

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:16.920
but it has a big impact.

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:18.545
This is going to save
us half a million

00:18:18.545 --> 00:18:21.720
dollars every single year and is
a product that now really takes

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:23.818
only a couple of days.

00:18:23.818 --> 00:18:25.610
Because of the variety
of imaging equipment

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:28.130
we have at IDEXX, we really
have hundreds of different types

00:18:28.130 --> 00:18:29.650
of problems we need to solve.

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:33.230
And AutoML is going to
make that a lot easier

00:18:33.230 --> 00:18:35.990
for us to solve these things.

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:39.376
Cool, I am going to hand
it off now back to Google.

00:18:39.376 --> 00:18:41.866
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:42.558 --> 00:18:44.100
FRANCISCO URIBE:
Awesome thanks a lot

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:48.790
Ben for the awesome use case and
our great partnership together.

00:18:48.790 --> 00:18:50.420
So now we're going
to have Arie Meir.

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:52.540
He's a product lead for
the Cloud Healthcare Team.

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:55.400
And he's going to give
us the vision of Google

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:57.240
for health care in the Cloud.

00:18:57.240 --> 00:18:59.830
ARIE MEIR: Thanks, Francisco.

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:01.460
Thank you, folks.

00:19:01.460 --> 00:19:05.110
So I think my colleagues have
presented here how information

00:19:05.110 --> 00:19:06.370
is important in health care.

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:10.010
And the value of information
helps make better decisions.

00:19:10.010 --> 00:19:13.528
So I want to share a little
story that kind of brought me

00:19:13.528 --> 00:19:14.820
into the Cloud Healthcare Team.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:17.620
So as Francisco mentioned,
I'm a product manager

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:19.670
within Cloud
Healthcare, which is

00:19:19.670 --> 00:19:21.430
a team that is in
Google Cloud that's

00:19:21.430 --> 00:19:25.420
focusing on industry-specific
cloud capabilities.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:27.940
And I'll talk a little
bit more specific.

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:31.057
So I happened to move
apartments a couple of times

00:19:31.057 --> 00:19:32.140
over the last three years.

00:19:32.140 --> 00:19:33.598
And every time I
move an apartment,

00:19:33.598 --> 00:19:36.440
I like to find a dentist
that lives where I live.

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:38.500
Like, I just like
that convenience.

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:39.910
And the thing is
that when you go

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:41.993
to a new dentist, the first
thing they want to do,

00:19:41.993 --> 00:19:44.320
they want to give you a
full x-ray of mouth, right?

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:45.820
I don't know, I
don't love that idea

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:47.710
because I'm getting
more radiation.

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:49.960
The other thing is I
have to pay for it.

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:54.310
And so I ask the new dentists,
well, why don't you just

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:55.437
connect to my old dentist?

00:19:55.437 --> 00:19:57.520
Because I had my x-rays
there like six months ago.

00:19:57.520 --> 00:19:59.740
And just bring the
information there.

00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:01.540
It's like oh, I cannot connect.

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:02.800
We're on a different system.

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:05.650
It's like OK, so I decided
before we do the x-ray,

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:07.510
let me go to the old dentist.

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:09.340
And I'll take that
x-ray on the CD, right?

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:11.230
So I found myself-- he
didn't have the CDs.

00:20:11.230 --> 00:20:13.750
I actually had to
find that digital CD.

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:15.910
Apparently they have this
thing, medical grade CDs

00:20:15.910 --> 00:20:17.580
that cost $20 a pop.

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:20.320
It's like more expensive
than the CD-ROM drive.

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:21.190
And then I bring it.

00:20:21.190 --> 00:20:23.642
He burns it on this CD and
I bring to my new doctor.

00:20:23.642 --> 00:20:24.850
And he says I cannot take it.

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:25.630
It's like, why not?

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:27.172
He's like well, It's
liability issue.

00:20:27.172 --> 00:20:29.230
Maybe somebody tampered
with your pictures.

00:20:29.230 --> 00:20:32.122
And then I cannot
operate based on that.

00:20:32.122 --> 00:20:33.080
I can't make decisions.

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:36.670
So I was like OK, so I got
the extra dose of radiation.

00:20:36.670 --> 00:20:38.890
I had to pay for it
because my insurance didn't

00:20:38.890 --> 00:20:41.880
cover more than the one set
of x-rays within six months.

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:44.590
But it just made me think
of how broken the system is

00:20:44.590 --> 00:20:48.890
in terms of IT or information
flow in the health care space.

00:20:48.890 --> 00:20:51.610
The other little piece
I want to share with you

00:20:51.610 --> 00:20:55.750
is as this article was
written by a medical doctor

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:59.860
and also a PhD in economics
by the name of Anupam Jena.

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:02.700
So this gentleman is a
Harvard medical doctor.

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:05.260
The only reason I mention
that he's a PhD in economics

00:21:05.260 --> 00:21:08.920
is that we can kind of assume
that he knows how to design

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:11.080
and run an experiment, right?

00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:14.320
And the experiment that
he describes in this paper

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:15.190
was very simple.

00:21:15.190 --> 00:21:21.130
So he noticed that in the US,
there are 30,000 cardiologists.

00:21:21.130 --> 00:21:27.100
And once a year, 70% of them
or 21,000 of those 30,000

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:30.140
go to this conference
that takes a week.

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:34.300
So basically, 70% of arguably
a very important doctor

00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:38.110
is out of their hospital,
not seeing patients.

00:21:38.110 --> 00:21:40.600
Question is, what happens
to these patients?

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:43.550
Who thinks that the
patients get better?

00:21:43.550 --> 00:21:46.190
Who thinks patients get worse?

00:21:46.190 --> 00:21:49.490
Well, the data shows that the
patients actually get better.

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:52.220
Mortality goes down when
the doctors are away.

00:21:52.220 --> 00:21:54.703
And I'm bringing-- you can
read the details there.

00:21:54.703 --> 00:21:56.120
But it's kind of
makes you wonder,

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:58.730
what's going on here, right?

00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:00.170
I have some theories.

00:22:00.170 --> 00:22:03.170
But what this data suggests
that the decisions that

00:22:03.170 --> 00:22:06.590
are made in the context
of health care systems

00:22:06.590 --> 00:22:09.320
are not optimized
necessarily for the benefit

00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:10.158
of the patients.

00:22:10.158 --> 00:22:12.200
And this is not the fault
of any specific doctor.

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:14.690
it's just the incentives
are complex, right?

00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:15.500
There is the payer.

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:16.417
There is the provider.

00:22:16.417 --> 00:22:18.050
There is a patient.

00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:21.670
And sometimes it's not aligned
with the benefit of the people

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:23.280
it should serve.

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:26.330
So as Google, we're trying
to think OK, we're definitely

00:22:26.330 --> 00:22:30.220
not coming from a place we
can-- oh, this will solve it,

00:22:30.220 --> 00:22:32.040
IT will solve all of this.

00:22:32.040 --> 00:22:35.680
But I think our strength is
around organizing information.

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:37.810
So we see our team's
mission is going

00:22:37.810 --> 00:22:40.590
to organize the world's
health care information

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:43.740
and help make better
decisions, help our customers

00:22:43.740 --> 00:22:50.010
and our partners make better
decisions for health care.

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:52.230
So what we do in
Cloud Healthcare,

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:55.950
we build tools that
help ingest and manage

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:57.450
information for
health care, which

00:22:57.450 --> 00:23:02.700
means EHR data, medical imaging
data, genomics data, dictation

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:04.740
data, device data.

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:07.840
And then enable
providers, enable vendors,

00:23:07.840 --> 00:23:09.930
enable our partners
and customers

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:13.230
to develop applications on
top of this data like the one

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:16.890
that you saw presented
by IDEXX, by Ben.

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:19.500
So in this short talk,
I want to give you

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:22.140
a little bit of a highlight
of how we are thinking

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:24.100
about our product
development roadmap

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:27.420
and where we are thinking
of going with this mission.

00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:31.630
And the one way to slice it
would be in three layers.

00:23:31.630 --> 00:23:34.337
So the first layer is
the data platform layer.

00:23:34.337 --> 00:23:35.920
And I'll talk a
little bit about this.

00:23:35.920 --> 00:23:38.940
But the idea here
is that before you

00:23:38.940 --> 00:23:43.050
can use all of these cool tools
like AutoML, like Cloud Machine

00:23:43.050 --> 00:23:45.850
Learning Engine, like
big data analytics--

00:23:45.850 --> 00:23:48.427
which is, for example,
a tool like BigQuery--

00:23:48.427 --> 00:23:50.010
before you can use
all of these tools,

00:23:50.010 --> 00:23:52.620
you need to organize your
data in a way that allows you

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:54.390
to use these tools effectively.

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:56.460
Otherwise, as Ben
mentioned, the data comes

00:23:56.460 --> 00:23:57.780
and it's kind of a mess.

00:23:57.780 --> 00:23:59.740
Sometimes the
metadata is missing.

00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:01.470
Sometimes the metadata is wrong.

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:03.030
And it's very
difficult to bring into

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:06.030
a normalized, standard form.

00:24:06.030 --> 00:24:08.160
The other piece is once
your data is in the cloud,

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:09.160
what can you do with it?

00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:10.920
Like what are the
types of cloud services

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:14.370
that you can use so that you can
focus your application on what

00:24:14.370 --> 00:24:17.280
really sets you apart?

00:24:17.280 --> 00:24:19.740
And then the last piece
is the actual applications

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:20.470
on top of it.

00:24:20.470 --> 00:24:23.590
So this is like three layers
to think about their platform.

00:24:23.590 --> 00:24:28.680
And I'll go over in details
about each one of them.

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:32.420
So this is kind
of a high level--

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:34.880
uber high level--
architecture slide

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:39.440
that describes how a
typical medical imaging can

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:42.660
work in the context of a cloud.

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:44.780
So you have some
sort of a device

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:46.310
that produces medical images.

00:24:46.310 --> 00:24:48.140
So on the left here,
you see something

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:50.480
that resembles an ultrasound.

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:53.240
Now, the data would be
ingested into the cloud

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:56.600
through a data layer
platform product we have

00:24:56.600 --> 00:24:59.030
called Cloud Healthcare API.

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:02.330
And then once the data is in
the cloud, it can be normalized.

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:06.020
It can be visualized using
commercial or open source

00:25:06.020 --> 00:25:06.980
viewers.

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:10.860
And it can be used for analysis
or training of AI models.

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:14.070
So if you think about what
happens in a typical--

00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:16.040
this is an example from
medical imaging world

00:25:16.040 --> 00:25:18.050
because we're talking
about vision--

00:25:18.050 --> 00:25:20.630
what happens typically
is the image is acquired,

00:25:20.630 --> 00:25:22.700
then it's being
pre-processed, then

00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:24.740
it's being presented
to the physician that

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:28.070
makes the decisions, and
then it's being archived.

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:31.250
So these are kind of the
different pieces represented

00:25:31.250 --> 00:25:32.870
here.

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:35.960
And the other piece that's
relevant to the data

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:39.090
is our effort around
public data sets.

00:25:39.090 --> 00:25:42.680
So if you're a
researcher, then typically

00:25:42.680 --> 00:25:45.710
what you're interested in
doing is testing hypotheses.

00:25:45.710 --> 00:25:48.200
You might have a
hypothesis of I have

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:52.250
an algorithm idea to detect,
let's say, kidney stones.

00:25:52.250 --> 00:25:54.980
I need a way to test this
hypothesis effectively.

00:25:54.980 --> 00:25:58.220
And then once I test it, I want
to be able to commercialize it.

00:25:58.220 --> 00:26:01.440
I want to be able to integrate
it in a clinical workflow.

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:04.220
So the first piece for
testing hypotheses--

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:06.140
we saw that this creates
a lot of bottleneck.

00:26:06.140 --> 00:26:08.540
And a lot of
researchers are doing

00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:11.370
a lot of IT work and
sort of doing research.

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:15.380
So what we have done, we
host a set of data sets

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:16.880
that are already public.

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:19.710
We just simplified access
to these data sets.

00:26:19.710 --> 00:26:23.180
So if you have, for
example, a research

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:26.660
project around genomics,
or medical imaging, or EHR,

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:27.810
looking into our platform.

00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:29.477
You might be able to
find data sets that

00:26:29.477 --> 00:26:31.220
would help you
simplify the testing

00:26:31.220 --> 00:26:32.480
of the hypotheses process.

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:37.702
Once the data is in the cloud,
what can you do with it?

00:26:37.702 --> 00:26:40.327
And it depends really on what do
you want to do with it, right?

00:26:40.327 --> 00:26:42.900
At the end of the day, cloud
is a collection of components.

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:45.330
And you can build an
application or solution

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:47.040
that meets your needs.

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:50.070
Some examples I'll bring
from what we see in the field

00:26:50.070 --> 00:26:53.060
from talking to
customers or partners.

00:26:53.060 --> 00:26:56.670
And on the analytics, two
buckets in this slide.

00:26:56.670 --> 00:26:58.770
On the left side you see
that analytics use cases.

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:01.650
On the right side, kind of
machine learning applications.

00:27:01.650 --> 00:27:04.650
So BigQuery is our
enterprise data warehouse

00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:06.930
tool that can be used
in a very versatile way

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:08.790
to analyze huge
quantities of data,

00:27:08.790 --> 00:27:10.620
so petabytes of information.

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:12.480
I'll just give you
one data point.

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:16.170
So BigQuery is the tool that
is used by Google to analyze

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:18.470
search traffic patterns.

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:21.720
So if you think about the
corpus of data behind Search,

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:25.050
this is a tool that can
analyze petabyte scale

00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:27.370
without any constraints.

00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:31.110
Now, the way we see people
using this in health care

00:27:31.110 --> 00:27:33.220
varies a lot by the
nature of the application.

00:27:33.220 --> 00:27:36.420
For example, some folks
must have flown into here.

00:27:36.420 --> 00:27:40.960
So you must have seen in the
airport when they ask you, hey,

00:27:40.960 --> 00:27:44.430
who here has flexibility to
stay and take a longer-- like,

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:45.240
a later flight?

00:27:45.240 --> 00:27:47.460
We'll give you, let's say,
a voucher for a hotel.

00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:48.900
You can see the city.

00:27:48.900 --> 00:27:51.510
Now, this happens
because the airlines,

00:27:51.510 --> 00:27:53.807
they don't like to have
empty seats in the airplane

00:27:53.807 --> 00:27:54.640
for obvious reasons.

00:27:54.640 --> 00:27:56.070
So they double book it.

00:27:56.070 --> 00:27:58.870
The problem is you,
sometimes in health care,

00:27:58.870 --> 00:28:02.850
find similar resources that are
very high precious commodity--

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:06.210
like for example, an
MRI machine or even

00:28:06.210 --> 00:28:09.570
a specific expert, a
specific specialist,

00:28:09.570 --> 00:28:13.650
you prefer to overbook and have
maybe the patient wait longer

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:16.410
than having a slot
that is not utilized.

00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:19.830
So we see customers that are
using the data that they gather

00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:22.470
from different parts of
their health care system

00:28:22.470 --> 00:28:25.170
to try and predict no shows.

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:27.375
Or another example I'll
give you is something

00:28:27.375 --> 00:28:29.850
called procedure leakage.

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:34.050
Leakage happens when a
patient for example shows up

00:28:34.050 --> 00:28:36.690
and let's say they're
about to do a surgery.

00:28:36.690 --> 00:28:37.780
It's a planned surgery.

00:28:37.780 --> 00:28:40.740
So there is typically
a preoperative imaging.

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:42.985
They will do this
exploratory, make sure that--

00:28:42.985 --> 00:28:44.610
as Ben mentioned,
you want to make sure

00:28:44.610 --> 00:28:47.310
that the animal, or
the human in this case,

00:28:47.310 --> 00:28:50.170
is capable to undergo the
procedure, everything is fine.

00:28:50.170 --> 00:28:52.620
But then if you are a large
health care institution,

00:28:52.620 --> 00:28:54.570
you see that the
patient chose to do

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:56.820
the imaging in your
institution but then

00:28:56.820 --> 00:28:59.430
they chose to do the
surgery somewhere else.

00:28:59.430 --> 00:29:02.340
And the way the economics
of health care work,

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:04.590
it actually might be
the imaging procedure

00:29:04.590 --> 00:29:07.590
you might be doing at a cost.

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:11.670
But the surgery procedure is the
one that your hospital actually

00:29:11.670 --> 00:29:12.740
makes revenue, right?

00:29:12.740 --> 00:29:15.900
So that's kind of the
business side of health care.

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:18.450
And then that's a problem
because as a hospital,

00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:21.120
you need to better
understand what causes that.

00:29:21.120 --> 00:29:24.590
Are your surgeons maybe
providing different service?

00:29:24.590 --> 00:29:27.300
Do you need to increase
the cost of your imaging?

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:29.543
This is the type of
business intelligence

00:29:29.543 --> 00:29:30.960
that you want to
get from the data

00:29:30.960 --> 00:29:33.000
that you're collecting
from your system.

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:37.170
So this is another example of
analysis on large scale data

00:29:37.170 --> 00:29:38.650
that you can do.

00:29:38.650 --> 00:29:40.570
De-identification.

00:29:40.570 --> 00:29:43.080
So that's the thing
I really liked

00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:47.270
about working with medical
images data in the animal

00:29:47.270 --> 00:29:49.440
spaces, you don't need
to worry about PHI.

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:52.200
So PHI stands for protected
health care information.

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:54.752
In my understanding, there is
no HIPPA for animal pictures.

00:29:54.752 --> 00:29:56.460
Which is fun because
you can take selfies

00:29:56.460 --> 00:29:58.170
with your patients.

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:00.220
In the human world, it
doesn't work that way.

00:30:00.220 --> 00:30:03.390
So you have to be, of course,
very careful what information

00:30:03.390 --> 00:30:06.610
is shared, like in
collaborations, for example.

00:30:06.610 --> 00:30:09.150
If you have a large
academic medical center,

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:12.150
typically you have the clinical
entity that sees patients,

00:30:12.150 --> 00:30:13.038
collects the data.

00:30:13.038 --> 00:30:14.830
And then you have the
research part, right?

00:30:14.830 --> 00:30:18.540
Like these researchers are
trying to increase the quality,

00:30:18.540 --> 00:30:21.750
develop new procedures,
develop new operations,

00:30:21.750 --> 00:30:23.520
develop new diagnostic methods.

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:27.000
And they use some of this
data from the clinical side.

00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:30.180
But for this research
you have to be

00:30:30.180 --> 00:30:32.170
mindful of what data is used.

00:30:32.170 --> 00:30:34.560
So this
de-identification process

00:30:34.560 --> 00:30:38.160
is very critical to enable
research in institutions.

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:40.980
Now, de-identification
is a hard problem.

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:42.490
I'll give you an example.

00:30:42.490 --> 00:30:44.670
Every time I present the
tools that we have around

00:30:44.670 --> 00:30:47.670
de-identification, I get the
question, well, can your tool,

00:30:47.670 --> 00:30:48.810
for example--

00:30:48.810 --> 00:30:51.570
it was a very specific question
that I got until we actually

00:30:51.570 --> 00:30:53.880
realized where it came
from-- can your tool detect

00:30:53.880 --> 00:30:56.690
the serial number of
a pacemaker in a CT?

00:30:56.690 --> 00:30:58.750
It's like whoa,
that's very specific.

00:30:58.750 --> 00:31:00.030
It turns out it can, right?

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:01.450
Because when you
have a pacemaker,

00:31:01.450 --> 00:31:02.960
it has a unique serial number.

00:31:02.960 --> 00:31:07.180
That serial number comes in a
CT image that scans the patient.

00:31:07.180 --> 00:31:10.560
And that is used to
identify who that person is.

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:13.200
So that's kind of like, OK,
you didn't think about it.

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.570
But the tool actually
does work here.

00:31:15.570 --> 00:31:17.670
Now the next question
was, can your tool

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:22.200
detect a name of a patient
written on a grain of rice

00:31:22.200 --> 00:31:26.202
in foreign language that was
like, worn, on her necklace.

00:31:26.202 --> 00:31:27.160
So this is a real case.

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:28.660
They actually showed
me the picture.

00:31:28.660 --> 00:31:31.000
Our tool didn't work
well for this case.

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:35.240
But so didn't the human
operators or any other tools.

00:31:35.240 --> 00:31:38.860
So I'm bringing this example
to mention that it's hard.

00:31:38.860 --> 00:31:42.740
It's hard to build tools
that are working perfectly.

00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:44.260
But these tools are
evolving because

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:47.020
of the advanced technology and
the AI that's kind of embedded

00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:47.840
in these schools.

00:31:47.840 --> 00:31:49.300
So we're making progress.

00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:52.000
And the benefit of
using cloud based tools

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:56.440
is that they evolve without you
having to deploy automatically.

00:31:56.440 --> 00:31:58.640
And of course there are
some questions around OK,

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:00.310
so how do you maintain
version controls?

00:32:00.310 --> 00:32:02.080
And we have some
thoughts about that we'd

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:04.860
love to share with you as well.

00:32:04.860 --> 00:32:06.810
Natural language
processing-- so this

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:08.370
is a huge umbrella of tools.

00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:10.720
And it really depends
what you want to do there.

00:32:10.720 --> 00:32:14.000
And we have some research
efforts in that space.

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:16.380
And for example, I'll give
you a couple of use cases

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:18.690
just to stimulate thinking.

00:32:18.690 --> 00:32:21.060
Imagine a large
health care provider

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:24.300
that reviews thousands
of patients every day.

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:27.840
Now, when a radiologist
writes a report.

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:29.640
They typically
look for the thing

00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:31.200
that the patient
was referred for.

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:35.850
Like for example, if the patient
was referred for brain bleed

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:39.450
inspection, the radiologist
is not looking for cancer

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:42.040
because that's what the
physician was telling him, hey,

00:32:42.040 --> 00:32:43.830
you should look for this.

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:47.097
And in some cases, since
you already have the person

00:32:47.097 --> 00:32:49.680
and you have the information in
front of you, you might alert,

00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:51.540
say hey, you need
to look into this.

00:32:51.540 --> 00:32:55.260
Because I wasn't looking for
it, but it's there in the data.

00:32:55.260 --> 00:32:57.990
So NLP is something
that can be used

00:32:57.990 --> 00:33:00.540
as well as image analysis
to try and detect

00:33:00.540 --> 00:33:04.170
these incidental findings
or critical findings,

00:33:04.170 --> 00:33:07.350
and help organizations
implement certain protocols.

00:33:07.350 --> 00:33:10.560
Like for example, maybe you're
looking for brain cancer.

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:12.910
And you want to rule that
out, that doesn't happen.

00:33:12.910 --> 00:33:14.490
But on the other
hand, you do see

00:33:14.490 --> 00:33:17.760
there is a bleed
that is suspicious

00:33:17.760 --> 00:33:20.730
that you want to respond
to within a few minutes

00:33:20.730 --> 00:33:22.570
because that might
be life threatening.

00:33:22.570 --> 00:33:26.040
So this is an example
where NLP can play a role

00:33:26.040 --> 00:33:29.280
to identify what the
radiologist or the doctor

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:32.550
writes in their report,
and start a new process

00:33:32.550 --> 00:33:35.950
inside your IT system.

00:33:35.950 --> 00:33:38.500
Labeling-- so Ben was
talking about the importance

00:33:38.500 --> 00:33:39.090
of labeling.

00:33:39.090 --> 00:33:41.410
Anytime you want to train
any kind of AI model,

00:33:41.410 --> 00:33:43.070
you need to basically teach it.

00:33:43.070 --> 00:33:46.690
Labeling is the way
that you teach AI models

00:33:46.690 --> 00:33:49.390
in a supervised learning
kind of environment.

00:33:49.390 --> 00:33:53.180
We have a set of tools that
enables you to label data.

00:33:53.180 --> 00:33:55.570
And we also have published
some interesting research

00:33:55.570 --> 00:33:57.520
on adjudication.

00:33:57.520 --> 00:33:59.140
Like if you think
about it, let's say

00:33:59.140 --> 00:34:00.520
you want to detect
kidney stones,

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:02.420
just for a simple example.

00:34:02.420 --> 00:34:06.820
So one simplistic approach would
be well, have a set of images.

00:34:06.820 --> 00:34:10.420
Show these sort of images to
us a radiologist or someone

00:34:10.420 --> 00:34:12.219
who can label them effectively.

00:34:12.219 --> 00:34:15.190
Basically you can
simplistically think OK, I

00:34:15.190 --> 00:34:17.350
show this image to
one doctor, and they

00:34:17.350 --> 00:34:19.690
would provide me the labels,
and that's good enough.

00:34:19.690 --> 00:34:21.670
In reality, what
happens if you do that,

00:34:21.670 --> 00:34:24.130
you bias your model
because you're basically

00:34:24.130 --> 00:34:27.580
encoding the brain of that
one particular doctor.

00:34:27.580 --> 00:34:31.030
So you need to balance it by
offering several opinions.

00:34:31.030 --> 00:34:34.280
And there is a way to do it in
a way that is cost-effective.

00:34:34.280 --> 00:34:38.300
The other example is think about
you have a labeling budget.

00:34:38.300 --> 00:34:42.230
So let's say you have only 1,000
pictures that you can label.

00:34:42.230 --> 00:34:44.469
But you have a pool of 10,000.

00:34:44.469 --> 00:34:46.780
Which thousand should you label?

00:34:46.780 --> 00:34:48.280
They're not all the same, right?

00:34:48.280 --> 00:34:51.489
Some of them might have
more discriminatory powers.

00:34:51.489 --> 00:34:53.440
So basically what it
means is that they

00:34:53.440 --> 00:34:55.400
have more signal to noise.

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:57.550
So when you're about
to train a model,

00:34:57.550 --> 00:35:00.100
you can get more insight
from these thousand

00:35:00.100 --> 00:35:04.340
than from the bottom thousand.

00:35:04.340 --> 00:35:08.360
On the machine learning side
of things, so AutoML obviously,

00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:11.240
this is a kind of a tool
that allows you very quickly

00:35:11.240 --> 00:35:14.690
without writing any code to
test out hypotheses to see

00:35:14.690 --> 00:35:16.610
how well your model performs.

00:35:16.610 --> 00:35:20.060
Some cases, you might be able to
use something that is produced

00:35:20.060 --> 00:35:21.830
by AutoML right off the. bat.

00:35:21.830 --> 00:35:24.680
In some cases, you use it
as an exploratory tool.

00:35:24.680 --> 00:35:26.590
You kind of gain some intuition.

00:35:26.590 --> 00:35:28.220
The data scientists
in the audience

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:30.370
would know that you have
lots of different tools.

00:35:30.370 --> 00:35:32.990
And depending on the
type of job you have,

00:35:32.990 --> 00:35:34.520
you need to choose
the right tool.

00:35:34.520 --> 00:35:37.605
What we see in practice is that
customers are experimenting

00:35:37.605 --> 00:35:38.480
with different tools.

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:40.940
Like AutoML is kind of
initial exploratory-- super

00:35:40.940 --> 00:35:44.510
quick and super easy
to try and understand

00:35:44.510 --> 00:35:47.703
the problem and the dimension
in which they should optimize.

00:35:47.703 --> 00:35:49.370
And sometimes they
use additional tools,

00:35:49.370 --> 00:35:51.290
like Cloud Machine
Learning Engine,

00:35:51.290 --> 00:35:55.100
which offers you a little bit
more knobs, more control power

00:35:55.100 --> 00:35:57.710
but requires a little bit
more expertise on the machine

00:35:57.710 --> 00:36:00.470
learning and data science side.

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:03.260
We are learning that one of the
challenges in the health care

00:36:03.260 --> 00:36:06.530
space is that a lot of companies
are building AI algorithms.

00:36:06.530 --> 00:36:09.443
And one of the challenges
is how do you--

00:36:09.443 --> 00:36:11.360
let's say if you're a
physician, a radiologist

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:13.230
for a medical imaging space--

00:36:13.230 --> 00:36:15.110
how do you evaluate
these models?

00:36:15.110 --> 00:36:16.670
Somebody says that
they have a model

00:36:16.670 --> 00:36:20.300
to detect the thoracic,
or skull, or extremity

00:36:20.300 --> 00:36:21.620
versus another model.

00:36:21.620 --> 00:36:23.720
How do you compare
their quality?

00:36:23.720 --> 00:36:26.210
Right now there is no
standards for that.

00:36:26.210 --> 00:36:27.830
Or somebody says
they have a model

00:36:27.830 --> 00:36:31.500
to detect lung
nodules for cancer.

00:36:31.500 --> 00:36:33.260
Another model is doing the same.

00:36:33.260 --> 00:36:35.310
Which one is better?

00:36:35.310 --> 00:36:37.620
So we are building
a tool that will

00:36:37.620 --> 00:36:42.440
be allowing you to evaluate and
compare these different models

00:36:42.440 --> 00:36:46.134
and choose the
right one for you.

00:36:46.134 --> 00:36:48.730
A couple of words
on applications.

00:36:48.730 --> 00:36:52.070
We are very much embracing
the open source world.

00:36:52.070 --> 00:36:55.760
And as Google, we
have a very open DNA.

00:36:55.760 --> 00:36:58.340
And we want to enable people
to use the tools that they're

00:36:58.340 --> 00:37:00.800
already used too.

00:37:00.800 --> 00:37:03.470
And then we have
on the health care

00:37:03.470 --> 00:37:07.430
space, and the medical imaging
space, and the vision domain,

00:37:07.430 --> 00:37:10.220
we have invested in
integrating some of these tools

00:37:10.220 --> 00:37:12.650
with our data back end.

00:37:12.650 --> 00:37:14.750
So what that means
is that you can

00:37:14.750 --> 00:37:17.150
take one of these
open source viewers

00:37:17.150 --> 00:37:20.180
or you can take a several-- we
have several commercial viewers

00:37:20.180 --> 00:37:21.020
through partners.

00:37:21.020 --> 00:37:23.930
We deliberately chose not
to build our own viewer

00:37:23.930 --> 00:37:27.470
because we want to focus on
the data platform and the cloud

00:37:27.470 --> 00:37:31.880
services that come with
it, and let the front end

00:37:31.880 --> 00:37:34.430
pieces to be driven by the
applications and the market

00:37:34.430 --> 00:37:35.150
demand.

00:37:35.150 --> 00:37:38.250
Because oftentimes, these things
tend to be highly specific.

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:41.060
For example, I just
learned this morning

00:37:41.060 --> 00:37:42.680
about a very
specific application

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:45.620
in the wound management niche--

00:37:45.620 --> 00:37:49.850
so a very specific viewer,
very specific workflow tool.

00:37:49.850 --> 00:37:52.040
And we want to give the
tools to our partners

00:37:52.040 --> 00:37:54.650
to build for these
very specific solutions

00:37:54.650 --> 00:37:58.340
because they have the domain
expertise to do that the best.

00:37:58.340 --> 00:38:03.022
This is an example of open
source de-identification tools.

00:38:03.022 --> 00:38:04.480
Because of the
reasons I mentioned,

00:38:04.480 --> 00:38:06.690
de-identification is a
hard problem to solve.

00:38:06.690 --> 00:38:10.050
But not only that, it's
a hard problem to--

00:38:10.050 --> 00:38:11.390
we have a solution today.

00:38:11.390 --> 00:38:13.520
You can try our DID solution.

00:38:13.520 --> 00:38:15.110
One question you
might ask is well,

00:38:15.110 --> 00:38:16.760
how good is your solution?

00:38:16.760 --> 00:38:18.860
Can you give me
some specifications?

00:38:18.860 --> 00:38:20.810
The challenge with
that is that in order

00:38:20.810 --> 00:38:25.070
to give you specifications, we
need to agree on a data set.

00:38:25.070 --> 00:38:28.130
And that data set has to have
PHI in it, protective health

00:38:28.130 --> 00:38:29.090
information.

00:38:29.090 --> 00:38:34.370
Now for this sensitive data, you
cannot have a publicly-facing

00:38:34.370 --> 00:38:36.540
data set with PHI.

00:38:36.540 --> 00:38:40.790
So the approach we took is we
integrated existing open source

00:38:40.790 --> 00:38:43.280
tools with our data back end.

00:38:43.280 --> 00:38:45.560
And we offered these
tools as well as

00:38:45.560 --> 00:38:49.490
our own tool to our users,
and customers, and partners

00:38:49.490 --> 00:38:52.130
basically to test it
out on your data set.

00:38:52.130 --> 00:38:54.020
So the idea is like,
here are three tools.

00:38:54.020 --> 00:38:56.360
Two of them are open
source, one of them is ours.

00:38:56.360 --> 00:38:57.482
See what works best.

00:38:57.482 --> 00:38:59.690
We are learning and practice
that people are actually

00:38:59.690 --> 00:39:03.120
trying to do a belt and
suspenders kind of approach.

00:39:03.120 --> 00:39:04.760
So they might do
actually all three

00:39:04.760 --> 00:39:09.530
in chain because they might
be used to some of these tools

00:39:09.530 --> 00:39:10.490
from the past.

00:39:10.490 --> 00:39:12.620
Sometimes there is an
issue of compliance

00:39:12.620 --> 00:39:15.350
because the open
source tools that

00:39:15.350 --> 00:39:18.560
have been approved by their
regulatory group inside,

00:39:18.560 --> 00:39:21.600
sometimes our tool takes
longer to get approved.

00:39:21.600 --> 00:39:23.420
So they basically
want to increase

00:39:23.420 --> 00:39:25.640
the level of confidence
they have in DID solution.

00:39:28.095 --> 00:39:29.720
So this is like a
couple of slides just

00:39:29.720 --> 00:39:34.370
to show you an example
of what we call a model

00:39:34.370 --> 00:39:35.990
browser, a model explorer.

00:39:35.990 --> 00:39:37.700
This is just a
mock at this point.

00:39:37.700 --> 00:39:40.460
This will be available
around mid-year.

00:39:40.460 --> 00:39:43.490
And this is a tool that would
allow you to select an AI

00:39:43.490 --> 00:39:48.180
model, to select a data set,
and apply the AI model on a data

00:39:48.180 --> 00:39:50.610
set, and be able to
compare or evaluate

00:39:50.610 --> 00:39:52.410
or reason about the results.

00:39:52.410 --> 00:39:55.713
So for example, think about
you could take an AI model,

00:39:55.713 --> 00:39:57.380
you could see that
the data set, and you

00:39:57.380 --> 00:40:00.020
can generate an AUC curve
like the ones you've

00:40:00.020 --> 00:40:01.235
seen before for AutoML.

00:40:05.020 --> 00:40:08.140
We also published a set of
tutorials that teach you

00:40:08.140 --> 00:40:13.900
how to essentially how to
build your own AI models on top

00:40:13.900 --> 00:40:16.000
of medical imaging data.

00:40:16.000 --> 00:40:19.258
And we have one that
focuses on AutoML as a tool.

00:40:19.258 --> 00:40:21.550
We have one, if you want a
little bit more flexibility,

00:40:21.550 --> 00:40:23.920
focused on Cloud
Machine Learning Engine.

00:40:23.920 --> 00:40:27.390
Again, the right tool for the
job is the name of the game

00:40:27.390 --> 00:40:27.890
here.

00:40:27.890 --> 00:40:30.490
So this is the link
where you can find it.

00:40:30.490 --> 00:40:34.180
And the example, we
chose to highlight

00:40:34.180 --> 00:40:38.560
in this case, very simple and
not really of clinical use.

00:40:38.560 --> 00:40:41.830
But it touches through
all the stations

00:40:41.830 --> 00:40:44.900
that you would need in order
to develop a real application.

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:47.180
So the example here
uses a public data

00:40:47.180 --> 00:40:49.840
set for mammography.

00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:54.790
And it basically creates a
classifier for breast density.

00:40:54.790 --> 00:40:58.350
You can see screenshots
from AutoML work.

00:40:58.350 --> 00:41:00.520
So this basically allows
you to evaluate the results

00:41:00.520 --> 00:41:02.680
and tune your model,
you can see hey,

00:41:02.680 --> 00:41:03.920
my model is not good enough.

00:41:03.920 --> 00:41:05.890
I need to get more data.

00:41:05.890 --> 00:41:07.690
A question we often
get is how much

00:41:07.690 --> 00:41:10.660
data do I need in order
to train a good model?

00:41:10.660 --> 00:41:12.430
And it's an empirical
question, right?

00:41:12.430 --> 00:41:14.263
Because if your data,
as I mentioned before,

00:41:14.263 --> 00:41:16.360
has a strong signal
to noise ratio,

00:41:16.360 --> 00:41:17.950
you might need less data.

00:41:17.950 --> 00:41:19.630
But if you keep
feeding it examples

00:41:19.630 --> 00:41:21.790
that are the same,
then obviously you

00:41:21.790 --> 00:41:24.010
need much more of that.

00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:28.055
So the rule of thumb
here-- and Francisco,

00:41:28.055 --> 00:41:28.930
maybe keep me honest.

00:41:28.930 --> 00:41:31.210
What we're hearing in
the medical imaging space

00:41:31.210 --> 00:41:33.310
is that for each
class you're trying

00:41:33.310 --> 00:41:36.640
to classify is between
1,000 and 10,000 samples--

00:41:36.640 --> 00:41:38.990
kind of a good starting point.

00:41:38.990 --> 00:41:40.450
So the advantage
of deep learning

00:41:40.450 --> 00:41:44.230
is that it reduces the need for
you to do feature engineering.

00:41:44.230 --> 00:41:47.830
But it comes at the expense of
having to produce more data.

00:41:47.830 --> 00:41:50.080
In a world where we produce
more data than we ever

00:41:50.080 --> 00:41:53.470
knew what to do with before,
I think it can be good news.

00:41:53.470 --> 00:41:55.240
The other way we're
trying to optimize it,

00:41:55.240 --> 00:41:57.700
how do we help you use
your labeling budget

00:41:57.700 --> 00:42:00.790
more effectively?

00:42:00.790 --> 00:42:03.400
Once you develop the
model, how do you deploy it

00:42:03.400 --> 00:42:04.900
in the clinical workflow?

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:06.340
So this is another piece.

00:42:06.340 --> 00:42:10.150
How do you take the output
of your AI predictions

00:42:10.150 --> 00:42:12.730
and bring it to the surface
so that your physician can

00:42:12.730 --> 00:42:13.960
make use of it?

00:42:13.960 --> 00:42:16.060
We have some sample
architectures about that.

00:42:16.060 --> 00:42:18.580
Come talk to us if this
is of interest to you.

00:42:18.580 --> 00:42:21.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

